WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.020 --> 00:00:03.380
Don Bluth's 1986 animated film An American Tail

00:00:03.380 --> 00:00:06.980
tells the story of a young Jewish-Russian immigrant, Fievel Mousekewitz,

00:00:06.980 --> 00:00:13.480
who is separated from his family during their journey to America while fleeing the Czar's Cossack pogroms in 1885.

00:00:13.760 --> 00:00:18.340
The film explores corrosive and false mid-century reactionary versions of the American myth

00:00:18.340 --> 00:00:22.840
that painted late 19th century America as gladly welcoming all immigrants.

00:00:23.080 --> 00:00:27.760
These revisionist myths largely propagated throughout the 1950s, 60s, and 70s

00:00:27.760 --> 00:00:32.000
in an attempt at bulwarking coalitions of Italian, Polish and Irish-Americans,

00:00:32.000 --> 00:00:36.620
ethnic groups that were routinely considered non-white prior to World War II,

00:00:36.620 --> 00:00:40.200
against the gains of black Americans in the civil rights movement.

00:00:40.200 --> 00:00:45.420
The newly bowdlerized version of American history played up the contemporary positives of the period,

00:00:45.480 --> 00:00:48.860
essentially taking Emma Lazarus's sonnet "The New Colossus"—

00:00:48.860 --> 00:00:53.280
"Give me your tired, your poor, your huddled masses yearning to breathe free"—

00:00:53.400 --> 00:00:57.900
and treating it as the popular consensus of late 19th century America,

00:00:57.900 --> 00:01:02.260
and not an equally radical statement then as it is now,

00:01:02.320 --> 00:01:05.760
opposed as it was by a substantial Nativist movement.

00:01:05.760 --> 00:01:11.280
An American Tail plays with these revisionist myths by having the Irish, Jewish, and Sicilian immigrant mice

00:01:11.409 --> 00:01:13.860
repeat the myth on the boat ride from Europe;

00:01:13.860 --> 00:01:19.440
"There are no cats in America, and the streets are paved with cheese."
- ♪ ["There Are No Cats In America"]

00:01:20.180 --> 00:01:26.380
Cats being synecdoche for racism, exploitation, and the predator class as a whole within the movie's metaphor.

00:01:26.580 --> 00:01:32.440
However, when the immigrant mice arrive in America, they discover that not only are there cats in America,

00:01:32.620 --> 00:01:35.660
America is full to the tits with cats.

00:01:35.840 --> 00:01:38.700
The mid-century myth is contrasted with the reality,

00:01:38.760 --> 00:01:43.120
that immigrants arrived to America to discover the predator class intact,

00:01:43.120 --> 00:01:49.660
ready to enforce societal stratification along racial and ethnic lines by whatever means necessary.

00:01:49.940 --> 00:01:55.160
In this regard, An American Tail is simply repeating the same lesson learned in "Dick Whittington and His Cat",

00:01:55.160 --> 00:01:58.300
when it was London's streets that were said to be paved with gold;

00:01:58.300 --> 00:02:02.820
Sir Whittington arriving in London to find not gold, literal or metaphorical,

00:02:02.820 --> 00:02:05.200
but grime, poverty, and cholera.

00:02:05.200 --> 00:02:08.680
However, An American Tail fumbles its metaphor at the end.

00:02:08.820 --> 00:02:14.960
While the climax of the film, where the cats are driven into the sea in an allusion to the biblical demon Legion,

00:02:14.960 --> 00:02:18.060
can be seen as aspirational or instructive,

00:02:18.060 --> 00:02:21.960
it is more likely to be read as its own form of revisionism.

00:02:22.300 --> 00:02:26.920
Though An American Tail suggests that the predator class was a reality of American life,

00:02:26.940 --> 00:02:29.120
confronting one form of revisionism,

00:02:29.220 --> 00:02:33.680
the emphasis is on "was", suggesting it as one of the past,

00:02:33.680 --> 00:02:36.820
a demon driven into the ocean a century earlier.

00:02:37.260 --> 00:02:41.120
♪ Now there are no cats in America ♪

00:02:41.120 --> 00:02:44.600
♪ We can do just as we please ♪♪
- Filly? Who's Filly?

00:02:44.600 --> 00:02:47.420
In succumbing to the necessity of a happy ending,

00:02:47.420 --> 00:02:54.160
An American Tail succumbs as well to white post-racial fantasies common following the passing of the Civil Rights Act.

00:02:54.500 --> 00:02:57.940
Still, while the ending of the film may be overly simplistic,

00:02:57.940 --> 00:03:01.500
An American Tail is unique among children's media

00:03:01.500 --> 00:03:07.400
for its willingness to depict the crowding, squalor, exploitation and abuse that immigrants faced.

00:03:07.600 --> 00:03:13.120
The film even includes commentary on the internal racial aggression of the underclass via the use of names,

00:03:13.120 --> 00:03:18.340
in how Fievel's ally Tony, a 2nd or 3rd-generation Italian immigrant himself,

00:03:18.340 --> 00:03:23.820
all but refuses to use Fievel's given name, preferentially calling him Filly.

00:03:23.820 --> 00:03:27.760
Fievel? Ooh, that name's gotta go!

00:03:27.860 --> 00:03:30.140
Hey, I'll tell ya what, Fi— Filly!

00:03:30.340 --> 00:03:33.720
An act that recreates the structural suppression of culture,

00:03:33.720 --> 00:03:36.520
seen when the Mousekewitzes arrive at immigration

00:03:36.520 --> 00:03:40.220
and Tanya's name is changed to Tilly by the immigration clerk.

00:03:40.600 --> 00:03:45.600
- MALE IMMIGRANT: Brovoloti Provalinsky.
- CLERK: Okay, Mister... Smith.

00:03:46.140 --> 00:03:49.200
TANYA: Papa, why did they change my name to Tilly?

00:03:49.680 --> 00:03:51.800
Aside from these thematic elements,

00:03:51.810 --> 00:03:55.600
the film itself is a bit of a drag, despite its short run time.

00:03:55.600 --> 00:03:58.820
Around the midpoint of the film, the story grinds to a halt

00:03:58.820 --> 00:04:04.220
and Fievel spends an inordinate amount of time futzing around with the vegetarian cat Tiger.

00:04:04.220 --> 00:04:10.680
While Tiger as an idea reflects the thematic beat that it's not species that defines evil, but behaviour,

00:04:10.680 --> 00:04:15.220
Tiger being a member of the predator class with no instinct for predation,

00:04:15.220 --> 00:04:21.100
his role in the film is largely to consume time with the comedy stylings of Dom DeLuise.

00:04:21.360 --> 00:04:22.820
D'you really think so?

00:04:23.000 --> 00:04:26.940
On the whole, though, as with much of Don Bluth's work in the 1980s,

00:04:27.080 --> 00:04:32.040
An American Tail is a flawed but unique and valuable piece of children's media,

00:04:32.300 --> 00:04:37.960
confronting a young audience with difficult, often complex ideas, emotions and realities.

00:04:39.780 --> 00:04:41.500
And 21 years later, it was made

00:04:41.500 --> 00:04:43.540
into one of the worst video games ever.

00:04:43.780 --> 00:04:50.480
♪♪

00:04:50.680 --> 00:04:54.400
Developed by Data Design Interactive and published by Blast! Entertainment,

00:04:54.400 --> 00:04:58.120
An American Tail was released for the PlayStation 2 in 2007,

00:04:58.120 --> 00:05:02.060
and it is one of the worst retail games I have ever encountered.

00:05:02.100 --> 00:05:04.220
[DRAMATICALLY READING AD COPY]
"Fievel Mousekewitz returns!"

00:05:04.220 --> 00:05:06.620
"6 huge missions!"

00:05:06.620 --> 00:05:12.880
"Steer Fievel inside a bubble, hitch a ride on the back of a pigeon, and escape from cats aboard a roller skate!"

00:05:12.880 --> 00:05:14.800
"4 bonus levels!"

00:05:14.800 --> 00:05:16.080
"Relive the movie!"

00:05:16.080 --> 00:05:20.800
"See the thrilling story unfold through classic clips from the animated movie!"

00:05:20.800 --> 00:05:22.020
Let's get started.

00:05:22.020 --> 00:05:25.840
Before the 6 huge missions and 4 bonus levels commence, we're treated to the menu,

00:05:25.840 --> 00:05:29.140
where a dead-eyed digital simulacrum of Fievel Mousekewitz stands

00:05:29.140 --> 00:05:31.540
as a grim portent of things to come.

00:05:31.540 --> 00:05:34.960
Entering a new game, we get a short clip from the opening of the film;

00:05:34.960 --> 00:05:37.260
Fievel running from the Cossack cat,

00:05:37.260 --> 00:05:40.760
and the family boarding the ship that will take them across the Atlantic to America.

00:05:41.060 --> 00:05:44.520
How, then, will they translate this into a game?

00:05:44.860 --> 00:05:46.380
It's Super Monkey Ball.

00:05:46.380 --> 00:05:49.620
It's— it's Super Monkey Ball on a pirate ship.

00:05:49.800 --> 00:05:54.400
Fievel is in a soap bubble, and it's Super Monkey Ball on a pirate ship.

00:05:54.780 --> 00:05:59.160
21 years after the movie went to theaters, some dudes turned it into a video game,

00:05:59.160 --> 00:06:01.780
and it's Super Monkey Ball on a pirate ship.

00:06:01.800 --> 00:06:06.500
There's not really a blow-by-blow to be had, because there's just not that much to the level.

00:06:06.500 --> 00:06:12.540
There's only two rooms; neither is very complex. They're just prolonged by the atrocious controls,

00:06:12.540 --> 00:06:17.200
making it simultaneously insultingly easy and comically difficult.

00:06:18.300 --> 00:06:22.400
After reaching the cheese in the second room, the player gets another short movie clip,

00:06:22.420 --> 00:06:26.760
and Fievel gets to move his soap bubble onwards to the first bonus level, which is...

00:06:27.740 --> 00:06:29.540
...just more Super Monkey Ball.

00:06:29.720 --> 00:06:33.800
This is actually optional anyway; you can just skip it from the menu.

00:06:33.800 --> 00:06:38.400
But if you do decide to try and complete it because you hate yourself,

00:06:38.400 --> 00:06:42.600
buckle up, because it is both far more difficult and complex than the previous level.

00:06:43.660 --> 00:06:48.220
Fievel needs to collect three cheeses that are scattered around a huge puzzle room,

00:06:48.220 --> 00:06:52.180
and in the process of getting them, you'll get to enjoy such thrilling moments as:

00:06:52.180 --> 00:06:54.560
thing you can't see that knocks you off,

00:06:54.560 --> 00:06:57.320
swinging barrels that utterly ruin the camera controls,

00:06:57.320 --> 00:07:02.740
a weird push maze that you need to reassemble after decoding the bizarre visual instructions,

00:07:02.860 --> 00:07:05.080
ever-present wind and boat noises...

00:07:05.080 --> 00:07:09.160
[WIND AND BOAT NOISES]

00:07:09.300 --> 00:07:13.800
...and falling into the water over and over and over

00:07:13.800 --> 00:07:16.580
and over and over again.

00:07:16.720 --> 00:07:20.220
I can't overstate just how awful these barrels are.

00:07:20.220 --> 00:07:23.000
Like, you can't— You just— You can't— You j...

00:07:23.000 --> 00:07:26.700
You can't— Just, come on, just, you just...

00:07:27.100 --> 00:07:28.580
[INFURIATED GRUMBLE]

00:07:28.840 --> 00:07:31.980
I get the feeling that they were just working on levels in a linear order,

00:07:31.980 --> 00:07:36.320
and then realized that the second level was impossible for their target demographic,

00:07:36.320 --> 00:07:41.380
so rather than change anything, they just called it a bonus level and made it skippable.

00:07:42.040 --> 00:07:47.500
In fact, on that note, all the other bonus levels are not just easier than this one,

00:07:47.500 --> 00:07:51.440
they are substantially easier than everything else in the game.

00:07:51.680 --> 00:07:56.840
The second proper level is a flight sim where Fievel rides on the back of Henri the pigeon,

00:07:56.840 --> 00:08:03.300
and the player must navigate the two of them through the busy New York harbour until Fievel is safely delivered to...

00:08:03.920 --> 00:08:06.120
the cheese floating over a pirate ship.

00:08:06.720 --> 00:08:09.720
Sounds easy enough, but here's the catch:

00:08:09.720 --> 00:08:13.220
Henri has a stamina bar that depletes comically fast,

00:08:13.220 --> 00:08:19.000
so every few seconds, the player needs to fly through floating birdseed to refill the stamina bar.

00:08:19.120 --> 00:08:24.060
Colliding with water, geese or any obstacles will also take off a huge chunk of stamina.

00:08:24.060 --> 00:08:29.420
There's actually several stretches where birdseed restores less stamina than it takes to fly between them,

00:08:29.420 --> 00:08:33.040
meaning that in addition to the obvious reactive challenges of the level,

00:08:33.040 --> 00:08:37.740
there's also the strategic challenge in that you're simultaneously fighting entropy.

00:08:40.140 --> 00:08:44.260
Henri controls so poorly that it's impossible to get all the seed,

00:08:44.260 --> 00:08:48.660
and the seed you can get is barely enough to get through to the end of the level.

00:08:48.700 --> 00:08:53.000
And don't go thinking that if you miss some seed, you can just loop back and get it;

00:08:53.000 --> 00:08:56.380
even trying is going to consume far more stamina than you'll get,

00:08:56.380 --> 00:09:02.000
so you're just dooming yourself to a slow loss, even if you fly the rest of the level perfectly.

00:09:02.320 --> 00:09:04.260
And if you hit something, you're pretty much done.

00:09:04.260 --> 00:09:08.320
Not only is the stamina cost just as much of a slow death as poor flying,

00:09:08.320 --> 00:09:14.260
there's a pretty good chance that you'll just get stuck to or inside of whatever you hit.

00:09:14.680 --> 00:09:18.600
And I should point out that you don't really steer per se.

00:09:18.600 --> 00:09:21.730
There's an ever-present lag between input and reaction,

00:09:21.730 --> 00:09:26.780
so for the most part, you're not steering as much as you're gently tapping the D-pad

00:09:26.780 --> 00:09:29.220
to make microcorrections to your course.

00:09:29.220 --> 00:09:35.400
The cherry on top of all that is that the level is so poorly designed, and the engine so poorly optimized,

00:09:35.400 --> 00:09:43.500
that gameplay actually slows down and gets less responsive as more and more assets are loaded.

00:09:43.700 --> 00:09:48.720
So not only is the path getting harder, with narrower passages and tighter turns,

00:09:48.740 --> 00:09:53.520
the fundamental interaction is getting more and more difficult

00:09:53.520 --> 00:10:00.420
as the game chugs to a crawl and inputs take longer and longer to register.

00:10:00.420 --> 00:10:02.720
And the whole time— the *whole time*—

00:10:02.780 --> 00:10:06.400
you've got this... "music"?
- ♪ [ULTRA-GENERIC POP/JAZZ BLARING]

00:10:06.720 --> 00:10:08.240
Is this music?

00:10:08.240 --> 00:10:10.800
I mean, yes, technically?

00:10:10.800 --> 00:10:13.080
Because the game was made as cheaply as possible,

00:10:13.080 --> 00:10:15.340
none of the music from the film was licensed,

00:10:15.340 --> 00:10:20.360
so rather than having the film's score, or any of the original songs, or even sound-alikes,

00:10:20.360 --> 00:10:27.760
the levels are all populated with bizarre, tonally inappropriate royalty-free pop/jazz music.

00:10:28.160 --> 00:10:31.620
It gets old *really* fast.

00:10:32.020 --> 00:10:34.980
Assuming you manage to eventually get to the cheese on the pirate ship,

00:10:34.980 --> 00:10:38.240
the next bonus level consists of Fievel floating down between laundry,

00:10:38.240 --> 00:10:40.180
like that one small moment in the movie.

00:10:40.180 --> 00:10:44.640
This is a bit more of what I would expect from a game made for six-year-olds;

00:10:44.640 --> 00:10:50.080
it's short and pretty easy, but you can still fail, so you need to at least try,

00:10:50.080 --> 00:10:52.020
but after the previous level...

00:10:52.340 --> 00:10:55.340
I'm not sure any six-year-old is ever getting here.

00:10:55.340 --> 00:10:58.760
Anyway, if you want the pro strats for this absurdly easy minigame,

00:10:58.760 --> 00:11:01.960
you can use 〇 and ✖ to brake or speed up,

00:11:01.960 --> 00:11:07.060
and I highly recommend you press ✖ to get this over with as fast as possible.

00:11:07.260 --> 00:11:12.980
For level 3, Fievel is stuck in the street and needs to collect 16 cheeses to advance while dodging humans and horses.

00:11:13.240 --> 00:11:16.240
The hardest part of this level is fighting against the camera angle;

00:11:16.400 --> 00:11:20.440
it's so close to the ground that the open manholes are basically invisible,

00:11:20.440 --> 00:11:24.300
and it's nigh impossible to accurately gauge depth.

00:11:25.680 --> 00:11:30.600
Like, here I feel like I should be on this cheese, but I'm not.

00:11:30.600 --> 00:11:32.160
I'm still in front of it.

00:11:33.180 --> 00:11:35.180
Anyway, it's still not difficult.

00:11:35.760 --> 00:11:39.560
The next bonus level is just more falling, except now it's at night.

00:11:39.560 --> 00:11:44.400
Between levels we get one more short movie clip that provides no story context whatsoever,

00:11:44.400 --> 00:11:47.960
but it's kinda relevant because we're about to head into a sewer.

00:11:47.960 --> 00:11:51.120
Level 4 is actually level 4-1;

00:11:51.120 --> 00:11:55.100
see, both this level and the next one take place in the sewers.

00:11:55.100 --> 00:11:58.580
But rather than just calling them levels 4 &amp; 5,

00:11:58.580 --> 00:12:02.900
someone decided they should be called 4-1 &amp; 4-2,

00:12:02.900 --> 00:12:06.160
so that the final level would be 5?

00:12:06.740 --> 00:12:12.340
But the back of the box says 6 levels, because even if you hyphenate two of them, they're still distinct levels.

00:12:12.540 --> 00:12:15.480
So why not just call them 4 &amp; 5?

00:12:15.880 --> 00:12:18.740
This game is a glimpse into madness!

00:12:19.120 --> 00:12:23.980
So for level 4-1, Fievel needs to ghost behind Warren T. Rat's accountant, Digit.

00:12:23.980 --> 00:12:27.840
It's the exact same idea as the stalking missions in Assassin's Creed,

00:12:27.840 --> 00:12:30.900
a game that incidentally came out a few months before this one,

00:12:31.040 --> 00:12:33.020
except, you know...

00:12:33.300 --> 00:12:34.200
bad.

00:12:34.520 --> 00:12:38.360
The added user interface objects showing if you're too close or too far

00:12:38.360 --> 00:12:45.200
somehow manage to look out of place in a game where nothing actually looks like it belongs.

00:12:45.840 --> 00:12:49.980
Just like almost everything else in the game, it's not actually very hard,

00:12:49.980 --> 00:12:53.380
but the unresponsive controls and frequent abrupt failures

00:12:53.380 --> 00:12:56.580
make it feel far more difficult than it really is.

00:12:56.580 --> 00:12:58.460
If you do decide to try this game out,

00:12:58.460 --> 00:13:03.660
I'll save you a lot of headache and let you know that Fievel has a double jump that's critical to beating the level;

00:13:03.660 --> 00:13:06.360
there's obstacles that are impossible to clear without it.

00:13:06.560 --> 00:13:09.460
Also, don't waste time trying to balance on pipes;

00:13:09.460 --> 00:13:11.360
Fievel's just going to fall off.

00:13:11.360 --> 00:13:16.780
You can get past just about every barrier by running up the slope of the wall, and then jumping over it.

00:13:16.780 --> 00:13:20.500
Once you make it to the cheese at the end of the level, it's on to the bonus level,

00:13:20.500 --> 00:13:23.800
which is more falling, just in a sewer.

00:13:23.800 --> 00:13:27.940
To be fair, this bonus level does escalate the difficulty from the previous levels,

00:13:27.940 --> 00:13:31.680
with the addition of moving spikes and pipes that spray poison gas.

00:13:31.680 --> 00:13:35.960
You actually need to use the brakes and acceleration strategically to avoid damage,

00:13:35.960 --> 00:13:40.160
but there's also a lot of bonus health available, so it's still pretty forgiving.

00:13:40.640 --> 00:13:46.580
Again, these bonus levels are the only part that really feels age-appropriate to the target market.

00:13:46.580 --> 00:13:48.160
Now, level 4-2.

00:13:48.160 --> 00:13:53.400
Honestly, after the flying level where the game slowly chokes and dies on its own assets,

00:13:53.400 --> 00:13:56.540
I didn't think the game had any more surprises for me.

00:13:56.540 --> 00:13:58.640
I mean, how could it top that?

00:13:58.640 --> 00:13:59.420
Well...

00:13:59.700 --> 00:14:03.220
How about a physics-based downhill racer

00:14:03.220 --> 00:14:09.100
that controls about as well as an actual mouse riding a roller skate down a sewer pipe?

00:14:09.100 --> 00:14:13.640
Just like Henri, the roller skate that Fievel rides is so temperamental that you're not steering

00:14:13.640 --> 00:14:19.220
as much as you're just gently nudging as a suggestion of where to go.

00:14:19.500 --> 00:14:26.000
Even being really light on the controls, it's still super easy to skid out and jack-knife the roller skate.

00:14:26.000 --> 00:14:29.980
Oh, and a bizarre detail that's not super obvious from the footage,

00:14:29.980 --> 00:14:35.000
but the directional controls are oriented to the pipe, not the skate,

00:14:35.000 --> 00:14:39.840
so once the skate turns more than 90 degrees sideways, the controls flip.

00:14:39.840 --> 00:14:42.160
Left becomes right and right becomes left,

00:14:42.160 --> 00:14:48.680
but as you try to turn back around, they flip again once you're mostly facing forwards,

00:14:48.680 --> 00:14:52.300
making it super easy to just get stuck sideways and stall out.

00:14:52.300 --> 00:14:58.200
Also, there's no accelerator; Fievel has no way of gaining velocity except rolling down a hill,

00:14:58.200 --> 00:15:04.660
so if the skate glances off a pipe and loses momentum, it's entirely possible to get stuck in a position

00:15:04.660 --> 00:15:11.120
where the skate just rolls back and forth between two small lumps in the terrain that it can't quite get over,

00:15:11.120 --> 00:15:14.880
until the cats catch up to Fievel and you fail back to the start of the level.

00:15:15.100 --> 00:15:17.720
Should you escape Warren T. Rat and make it to the cheese,

00:15:17.720 --> 00:15:21.520
it's on to the sixth and final level, level 5—

00:15:21.600 --> 00:15:26.580
an autoscroller where the player steers the Mouse of Minsk and chases the cats off the pier.

00:15:26.600 --> 00:15:29.960
And I have to say, I do not use that word lightly.

00:15:29.960 --> 00:15:32.180
The Mouse of Minsk may not steer well,

00:15:32.180 --> 00:15:36.580
but compared to Henri and the roller skate, it does steer.

00:15:36.580 --> 00:15:41.820
So, if you avoid enough boxes, and don't tip over or fall in the water, what do you get?

00:15:41.960 --> 00:15:47.280
"Congratulations! Fievel has saved New York from the cats and been reunited with his family!

00:15:47.280 --> 00:15:51.740
Maybe finally they can settle down and enjoy their new life in America..."

00:15:52.180 --> 00:15:53.680
Roll credits.

00:15:53.680 --> 00:15:56.440
That's it. Not even a movie clip.

00:15:56.500 --> 00:16:00.880
There are, in total, 87 seconds of the actual film in the game.

00:16:00.880 --> 00:16:05.160
[PITCH DECREASING]
"See the thrilling story unfold through classic clips from the

00:16:05.160 --> 00:16:07.120
ＡＮＩＭＡＴＥＤ　ＭＯＶＩＥ！"

00:16:07.120 --> 00:16:08.840
Now, many of you—

00:16:08.840 --> 00:16:10.920
connoisseurs of bad video games—

00:16:10.920 --> 00:16:15.600
you may be wondering why, nay, *how* you have never heard of this before,

00:16:15.660 --> 00:16:19.620
and it's because this game is surprisingly rare.

00:16:19.620 --> 00:16:24.760
It was only released in Europe, Australia &amp; New Zealand as a budget title for the PlayStation 2,

00:16:24.760 --> 00:16:30.420
at a time when retailers were quickly pivoting to the newly released PlayStation 3 and Xbox 360.

00:16:30.420 --> 00:16:34.300
So it wasn't exactly a massive distribution in the first place,

00:16:34.300 --> 00:16:37.300
and it's only available in PAL video format.

00:16:37.300 --> 00:16:41.540
So it's not impossible to find, but it's hardly cluttering up pawn shops

00:16:41.540 --> 00:16:43.920
like sports titles of years past.

00:16:43.920 --> 00:16:47.700
In fact, it was actually a bit of an ordeal for me to get a working copy.

00:16:47.700 --> 00:16:49.420
Just to add to the cheapness,

00:16:49.420 --> 00:16:52.360
the game is printed on CDs, not DVDs,

00:16:52.360 --> 00:16:56.740
which would explain why there's only a whopping 87 seconds of movie footage in the game.

00:16:56.740 --> 00:17:01.580
Now, being CD-based wasn't super uncommon for PlayStation 2 games,

00:17:01.580 --> 00:17:06.760
but they're somewhat notorious for not working properly, and this was the problem I was having.

00:17:06.760 --> 00:17:12.460
My PAL PlayStation would read DVD-based games just fine, but not An American Tail,

00:17:12.460 --> 00:17:15.380
which was the only CD-based game I had available.

00:17:15.380 --> 00:17:17.940
Now, just to explain this, when playing a CD-based game,

00:17:17.940 --> 00:17:21.200
the PlayStation 2 needs to adjust the focal distance of its laser

00:17:21.200 --> 00:17:26.700
and run the disc drive's motor at a much higher RPM than what is used for DVD games.

00:17:26.700 --> 00:17:29.700
As a result, the two most common points of failure

00:17:29.700 --> 00:17:32.920
are that the CD setting for the laser falls out of position,

00:17:32.920 --> 00:17:40.060
or the disc spins too fast and slips inside the disc tray because the stopper doesn't have a tight enough grip.

00:17:40.180 --> 00:17:45.040
A commonly recommended solution for disc slipping is what's called "the tape trick".

00:17:45.040 --> 00:17:50.200
The idea is to put a few short strips of tape on the top of the disc, giving the stopper extra grip.

00:17:50.200 --> 00:17:53.780
So I tried this, since it's a pretty simple home hack,

00:17:53.800 --> 00:17:55.660
and something went wrong.

00:17:55.660 --> 00:17:59.920
The tape got caught inside the drive, though it didn't damage the PlayStation at all,

00:17:59.920 --> 00:18:03.700
and when I took the game out of the tray, the tape had actually peeled off the label

00:18:03.920 --> 00:18:07.340
*and* the reflective aluminum layer underneath,

00:18:07.340 --> 00:18:12.140
because the game is printed on super cheap CDs where the label is screen-printed

00:18:12.140 --> 00:18:18.300
directly onto the back of the reflective layer, with no protective plastic or lacquer backing in between.

00:18:18.620 --> 00:18:22.680
This isn't unheard of; it's just the video game equivalent of a pulp novel

00:18:22.680 --> 00:18:27.400
printed on low-quality paper and bound with cheap glue that dries out and cracks.

00:18:27.400 --> 00:18:31.180
So the game is uncommon, it's region-locked, and it's fragile.

00:18:31.680 --> 00:18:33.420
And I have two copies now.

00:18:34.220 --> 00:18:35.560
Whatever one of them works.

00:18:35.700 --> 00:18:36.940
So...

00:18:41.380 --> 00:18:43.000
Why, Part 1:

00:18:43.000 --> 00:18:44.560
Why does this exist?

00:18:44.560 --> 00:18:46.140
The simple answer is money.

00:18:46.200 --> 00:18:49.160
I wish there were a deeper story here, but that's really it.

00:18:49.160 --> 00:18:54.300
The game is exactly what it looks like: a cheaply made product shoveled out at a budget price,

00:18:54.300 --> 00:18:58.740
using a recognizable franchise to draw at least a modicum of attention.

00:18:59.060 --> 00:19:04.740
Blast! Entertainment was a London-based publisher &amp; development studio focused on cheaply made budget games,

00:19:04.740 --> 00:19:09.560
usually leveraging an older, less popular, or forgotten movie license,

00:19:09.560 --> 00:19:12.800
like Water Horse: Legend of the Deep, Charlotte's Web,

00:19:12.880 --> 00:19:17.060
Lassie, Dr. Doolittle, Home Alone, Babe, Beverly Hills Cop,

00:19:17.220 --> 00:19:21.080
Jumanji, Xena: Warrior Princess and Top Gun;

00:19:21.260 --> 00:19:26.400
most of these games coming out years or decades after their respective properties.

00:19:26.540 --> 00:19:30.120
Data Design Interactive, similarly, was a shovelware developer,

00:19:30.120 --> 00:19:34.500
though licensed games were more the exception than the rule in their library.

00:19:34.720 --> 00:19:38.180
Like, you maybe remember them for Ninjabread Man,

00:19:38.180 --> 00:19:40.160
but probably not.

00:19:40.520 --> 00:19:43.080
According to producer Stewart Green's LinkedIn page,

00:19:43.080 --> 00:19:47.080
Data Design Interactive was the leading publisher of Nintendo Wii titles,

00:19:47.080 --> 00:19:51.220
with 28 titles developed and published in a single year.

00:19:51.440 --> 00:19:55.700
While he doesn't say what year that was, and I have no easy means to verify that information,

00:19:55.700 --> 00:19:57.860
I'm gonna take him at his word, because...

00:19:58.460 --> 00:20:00.540
why would you brag about that?

00:20:00.780 --> 00:20:02.740
The other producer, Mark Gemmell,

00:20:02.740 --> 00:20:07.000
currently works for PikPok making microtransaction-stuffed Cow Clicker games.

00:20:07.160 --> 00:20:08.040
Now...

00:20:08.380 --> 00:20:10.000
Why, Part Two:

00:20:10.200 --> 00:20:11.300
Why care?

00:20:11.540 --> 00:20:16.580
I wanted to share this with you, not because it's important or good or an underrated gem,

00:20:16.580 --> 00:20:19.280
but because it's none of those things.

00:20:19.400 --> 00:20:25.380
This game is bad. It's cheaply made, it's difficult to find, it's largely forgotten, it's not fun,

00:20:25.380 --> 00:20:28.800
and for all those reasons, it's likely to vanish entirely.

00:20:28.800 --> 00:20:31.000
And that's why I wanted to preserve it.

00:20:31.000 --> 00:20:34.220
I believe in the value of failed art;

00:20:34.220 --> 00:20:38.640
art that is driven by carelessness, by unchecked and untalented ego,

00:20:38.640 --> 00:20:41.140
by spectacularly low-stakes greed.

00:20:41.140 --> 00:20:45.360
It has a tendency to be novel, to be unpredictable,

00:20:45.360 --> 00:20:48.200
in a way that deliberate art never can.

00:20:48.200 --> 00:20:52.180
This is why it's so much fun to watch bad movies.

00:20:52.180 --> 00:20:55.480
No one would ever make this game on purpose.

00:20:55.480 --> 00:21:01.360
Something in the creative process needs to be fundamentally broken to get to this point.

00:21:01.500 --> 00:21:06.040
If you were going to sit down two decades later to make a game out of An American Tail

00:21:06.040 --> 00:21:10.100
because you actually cared about the movie, and you cared about making a game,

00:21:10.100 --> 00:21:16.080
you're not going to churn out a hodgepodge series of disconnected minigames that don't work well.

00:21:16.080 --> 00:21:22.020
It is not simply a lack of time or money that produces something like An American Tail the video game,

00:21:22.020 --> 00:21:25.320
but a profound lack of caring.

00:21:25.320 --> 00:21:29.880
The end product of that broken process isn't worth playing for its own merits,

00:21:29.880 --> 00:21:33.560
but it is worth playing because it's worth remembering.

00:21:33.760 --> 00:21:36.720
So, thank you all for coming along.

00:21:36.720 --> 00:21:40.420
I hope you all have shared in my epicaricacy,

00:21:40.420 --> 00:21:45.060
the joy of looking on something bad.

00:21:45.680 --> 00:21:49.880
[OBNOXIOUS TITLE SCREEN MUSIC]

